<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  SAND_SWIMMERS_MOD_1 / DEEP_SAND_WALKABLE_TERRAIN_1 — Deep Sand: a slow, punishing
  sand terrain that behaves like water for FISHING ONLY, without being water at all
  and without being impassable.

  2026-09-27 OWNER RULING SUPERSEDES "impassable like deep water", verbatim:
  "Deep sand pools are NOT unwalkable, just very slowly. And they should be an entire
  terrain type, not just small pools. Some in the Long Shade, Muchly in the
  Stillsand." `passability` below is now `Standable` (was `Impassable`); `pathCost`
  is UNCHANGED at 300 because that value was always just sitting inert behind the
  Impassable flag — see the pathCost note below for what it now actually does, and
  DEEP_SAND_WALKABLE_TERRAIN_1 for the biome-terrain-weighting half of this ruling
  (RM_LongShade.xml / RM_Stillsand_Biome.xml `terrainPatchMakers`).

  MECHANISM, verified against RimWorld 1.6 + Odyssey decompiled source (not guessed):
  - `TerrainDef.IsWater` (Verse/TerrainDef.cs:324) is `HasTag("Water")` — a TAG check,
    completely independent of `passability`. Any TerrainDef carrying the "Water" tag
    reads as water to every system that calls `.IsWater`, whether or not it is
    walkable (pathing avoidance, `Zone_Fishing.IsFishable`, `SwimPathFinder`,
    `JoyGiver_GoSwimming`, `WaterSource`, flood/explosion FX, etc.) — this is exactly
    why the fishing hookup below never needed to change.
  - `Zone_Fishing.IsFishable(cell)` (RimWorld/Zone_Fishing.cs) requires
    `cell.GetTerrain(map).IsWater` AND `cell.GetWaterBodyType(map) == FishType`, where
    `FishType` comes from the zone's `TerrainDef.waterBodyType` — a closed four-value
    enum, `Verse/WaterBodyType.cs` (None/Freshwater/Saltwater/Other); there is no "Sand"
    water body type, and adding one would need C#. Declaring `waterBodyType: Freshwater`
    here is what lets a fishing zone actually form fishable cells on Deep Sand, and
    NONE of this depends on passability — fishability and walkability are unrelated
    fields read by unrelated systems, which means this def's own PRIOR header (below,
    kept for provenance in git) had it backwards: passability never "rode" the Water
    tag; it is, and always was, its own explicit field.
  - FISHING WAS ACTUALLY BROKEN WHILE THIS WAS IMPASSABLE, and flipping passability is
    what fixes it: `Designator_ZoneAdd_Fishing.CanDesignateCell`
    (RimWorld/Designator_ZoneAdd_Fishing.cs:65) refuses ("CannotPlaceFishingZone") on
    any cell where `passability == Impassable` — a THIRD, separate passability check,
    on top of `IsWater`/`waterBodyType`. So the player could never have drawn a fishing
    zone on Deep Sand through the normal UI while it was Impassable, in any biome.
    `IsFishable`/`WorkGiver_Fish` never cared about passability; only the zone-ADD
    designator did, and only it blocked the whole feature.
  - This def is written from scratch rather than `ParentName="WaterBase"`, deliberately,
    to avoid inheriting water-only baggage (the `SoakingWet` traversedThought — a
    per-TerrainDef field, never set here, so it does NOT ride the Water tag; the
    `Map/WaterDepth` shader/`waterDepthMaterial`; `floodTerrain`) that reads wrong on
    sand. That reasoning is UNCHANGED by the passability flip.
  - THE ONE THING THAT DOES NOT GATE ON PASSABILITY, found while researching this
    ruling: `JoyGiver_GoSwimming`'s cell validator requires `IsWater` AND
    `x.Standable(pawn.Map)` — and `IntVec3.Standable` is exactly `Walkable` (i.e. not
    Impassable) plus a things-on-cell check. While Deep Sand was Impassable, Standable
    was always false and this joy activity could never target it; flipping to Standable
    makes it eligible, so WITHOUT A GUARD a bored colonist could be offered a
    recreational "go swimming" job on Deep Sand — `JobDriver_GoSwimming.
    CheckForSwimmingPose()` sets `job.swimming = true` purely from `IsWater`, no depth
    or WaterBase check, so the pawn would visibly swim-pose in sand. Guarded by
    `RM_NoRecreationalSwimExtension` below + `RM_Patch_NoRecreationalSandSwim.cs`
    (Harmony postfix on `JoyGiver_GoSwimming.TryGiveJob`, the ONLY production caller of
    `SwimPathFinder.TryFindSwimPath` — verified, its only other caller is a debug
    tool) — see that file's header for the full trace. This is exactly the "some OTHER
    path than WaterBase" case the item asked to rule out, and it was real.
  - `moveSpeedFactorByTerrainTag` (keyed on the "Water" TAG — e.g. `JOE_Cephalope`/
    `JOE_Landopus`'s own PawnKindDefs already carry a `Water` entry) will now also
    apply on Deep Sand for any pawnKind that has one. Checked, and this is CORRECT,
    not a bug: it is exactly the water-adjacent sand-swimmer speed boost those kinds
    were authored for.
  - Deliberately NOT patched: `SwimPathFinder` itself, and `Pawn.Swimming`/
    `DrawNonHumanlikeSwimmingGraphic` in general — those exist for the terrain's own
    sand-swimming predator and for genuine water elsewhere, and gating the one
    colonist-recreation entry point (above) is a complete fix without touching them.

  pathCost 300 is UNCHANGED from the original (inert) Impassable-era value, and is now
  load-bearing for the first time. Compared against the live def dump / vanilla source
  for the closest analogues: Marsh and WaterShallow both ship `pathCost 30` (a mild
  "wade through it" tax); WaterDeep ships `pathCost 300` too, but it is Impassable so
  that number is normally inert there as well. 300 is 10x Marsh/WaterShallow's wade
  cost — matching the description below ("sinks... slowly and completely") with a
  genuinely punishing crawl rather than a shallow-water wade, while remaining finite
  per the owner's ruling. `extraNonDraftedPerceivedPathCost`/
  `extraDraftedPerceivedPathCost` (180/18) already match the Marsh/WaterShallow/
  WaterDeep family exactly and needed no change.

  Engine/content split (NAMING_SCHEME_PLAN.md §1): this terrain is a pure mechanic —
  "punishing-but-walkable sand you can fish in" — with zero Star Wars flavor, so it is
  RM tier and lives here in ManyWaters (already the RM-tier "gathers all water
  effects/types" engine, MANYWATERS_GENERIC_SPLIT_1) rather than a new mod. It names
  no biome of its own, matching that mod's existing generic/biome-agnostic pattern.
  The Star-Wars-flavored fish family caught in some sitings is RSW tier (SWBestiary);
  a biome's own fishing hookup is that biome's own tier.

  SITING — this def is now painted as a real terrain REGION by
  `RM_LongShade.xml`/`RM_Stillsand_Biome.xml`'s own `terrainPatchMakers`
  (DEEP_SAND_WALKABLE_TERRAIN_1), not by a fixed pool siting list. The original
  Cracked-Lands-only fish-family siting note this header used to carry is UNCHANGED
  and untouched by this pass — see Patches/SandFishing_CrackedLands.xml's own header
  for that history; it targets a different biome than the two this ruling touches.

  ART — PLACEHOLDER. texturePath below points at the vanilla `Terrain/Surfaces/SoftSand`
  texture as a deliberate stand-in so this never renders magenta; a real "sunken,
  rippled sand pool, visibly deeper/darker than surrounding sand" texture is OWED
  (generating-rimworld-sprites skill) and is not part of this pass.

  ANTI-EXPONENTIAL: this def carries no yield lever of its own (that lives in a
  biome's own fishTypes/maxFishPopulation) — it only makes cells fishable, it does not
  make fish more common.
-->
<Defs>

  <TerrainDef ParentName="NaturalTerrainBase">
    <defName>RM_DeepSand</defName>
    <label>deep sand</label>
    <description>A pool of sand deep enough to swallow a person whole. The surface looks like any other dune, but there is nothing solid beneath it -- step out onto it and you sink, slowly, fighting for every stride. Nothing crosses deep sand quickly. Some things live in it.</description>
    <texturePath>Terrain/Surfaces/SoftSand</texturePath>
    <!-- PLACEHOLDER ART — see header. -->
    <edgeType>FadeRough</edgeType>
    <categoryType>Sand</categoryType>
    <renderPrecedence>353</renderPrecedence>
    <fertility>0</fertility>
    <natural>true</natural>
    <passability>Standable</passability>
    <!-- 2026-09-27 ruling: walkable, just very slowly. See header. -->
    <pathCost>300</pathCost>
    <avoidWander>true</avoidWander>
    <canFreeze>false</canFreeze>
    <changeable>false</changeable>
    <holdSnowOrSand>false</holdSnowOrSand>
    <extinguishesFire>true</extinguishesFire>
    <extraDeteriorationFactor>3</extraDeteriorationFactor>
    <extraNonDraftedPerceivedPathCost>180</extraNonDraftedPerceivedPathCost>
    <extraDraftedPerceivedPathCost>18</extraDraftedPerceivedPathCost>
    <waterBodyType>Freshwater</waterBodyType>
    <!-- Not literal water — Freshwater is the only bucket BiomeFishTypes offers that
         isn't briny; picked so a biome's own fishTypes has somewhere to hang its
         freshwater_Common/Uncommon catches. -->
    <filthAcceptanceMask>
      <li>None</li>
    </filthAcceptanceMask>
    <tags>
      <li>Water</li>
      <!-- The load-bearing tag: TerrainDef.IsWater == HasTag("Water"). Fishing
           (Zone_Fishing/WorkGiver_Fish/JobDriver_Fish) works unmodified off this tag
           alone, no Harmony patch needed — passability is a fully separate field, see
           header. -->
    </tags>
    <tools>
      <li>
        <label>sand</label>
        <capacities>
          <li>KickMaterialInEyes</li>
        </capacities>
        <hediff>SandInEyes</hediff>
        <!-- Reuses vanilla Sand/SoftSand's own hazard hediff rather than WaterInEyes. -->
        <cooldownTime>1.5</cooldownTime>
      </li>
    </tools>
    <statBases>
      <Beauty>-5</Beauty>
      <BeautyOutdoors>-3</BeautyOutdoors>
    </statBases>
    <modExtensions>
      <li Class="RimMandrake.FlowWorks.ManyWaters.RM_NoRecreationalSwimExtension" />
      <!-- Fishable "Water"-tagged ground, not literal swimmable water — keeps
           JoyGiver_GoSwimming from offering a recreational swim job here now that
           the terrain is Standable. See header and RM_Patch_NoRecreationalSandSwim.cs. -->
    </modExtensions>
  </TerrainDef>

</Defs>
